<p>In this network of 9 points, any two points that are linked have 1 linked point in common, forming a triangle. Any two points that <em>aren&#8217;t</em> linked have 2 linked points in common, forming a quadrilateral. Is such a pattern possible in a network of 99 points? In 2014 Princeton mathematician John Horton Conway offered $1000 for the answer to this question; so far the prize is unclaimed.</p>
